\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces  Charge distribution measured in the R3B MUSIC detector using predefined calibration parameters, after applying positional cuts on MWPC0 (located upstream of the ionization chamber) and requiring a coincident signal in the Start detector. The rise in counts for $Z \geq 7.2$ originates from pile-up events (see also Fig.~\ref  {fig:r3b_vs_twin_charge_def} in Appendix~\ref  {app:r3bmusic_pileup}), while the sharp cutoff at $Z \gtrapprox 7.35$ reflects the upper limit of the electronics\IeC {\textquoteright } dynamic range. The decrease in counts below $Z \lessapprox 4.8$ is caused by threshold settings applied in the detector readout. The high-$Z$ tail is attributed to imperfect pole\IeC {\textendash }zero cancellation in the detector electronics, whereas the low-$Z$ tail results from nuclear reactions occurring in upstream materials. Thick target run, 400 AMeV. \relax }}{58}{figure.caption.25}}
\newlabel{fig:r3bmusic_charge}{{22}{58}{Charge distribution measured in the R3B MUSIC detector using predefined calibration parameters, after applying positional cuts on MWPC0 (located upstream of the ionization chamber) and requiring a coincident signal in the Start detector. The rise in counts for $Z \geq 7.2$ originates from pile-up events (see also Fig.~\ref {fig:r3b_vs_twin_charge_def} in Appendix~\ref {app:r3bmusic_pileup}), while the sharp cutoff at $Z \gtrapprox 7.35$ reflects the upper limit of the electronics’ dynamic range. The decrease in counts below $Z \lessapprox 4.8$ is caused by threshold settings applied in the detector readout. The high-$Z$ tail is attributed to imperfect pole–zero cancellation in the detector electronics, whereas the low-$Z$ tail results from nuclear reactions occurring in upstream materials. Thick target run, 400 AMeV. \relax }{figure.caption.25}{}}
\newlabel{fig:r3bmusic_charge@cref}{{[figure][22][]22}{[1][58][]58}}
